# HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 only — no HTTP/3.
#
# Caddy serves h3 by default and advertises it with an `Alt-Svc` header, which
# tells the browser to use QUIC over **UDP** 443 for everything after the first
# request. That is normally faster and invisible. It is not invisible on a
# network that drops UDP 443, and plenty do — mobile carriers, corporate
# firewalls, and ISPs in a number of countries.
#
# What that looks like from the outside is deeply misleading: the first page
# loads over TCP and renders perfectly, then every `fetch` the page makes fails
# at the network layer. The browser reports it to JavaScript as a bare "Failed
# to fetch" — no status, no body, nothing naming a cause — while the server is
# healthy and answers `curl` from anywhere else without complaint. It cost a
# long hunt through CORS, certificates and endpoint names, none of which were
# ever wrong.
#
# The gain from h3 here is a few milliseconds on a dashboard nobody has open
# for long. The cost is a customer who cannot sign up and a message that points
# nowhere. Not a close call.
{
	servers {
		protocols h1 h2
	}
}

# DOMAIN comes from the environment (see docker-compose.yml).
#   DOMAIN=:80                 -> plain HTTP on the server's IP, for testing
#   DOMAIN=clips.example.com   -> automatic HTTPS via Let's Encrypt
#
# Uploads can be large videos, so the default request-body cap is lifted.
{$DOMAIN} {
	# Licence traffic goes to its own service. These paths are matched first
	# because the video app owns everything else under /api.
	# `/api/admin/*` as a whole rather than each endpoint by name: the previous
	# form listed them individually, and adding an admin endpoint without also
	# editing this line sends it to the video app, which answers 404 for a route
	# that exists and works. Every admin endpoint checks its own token.
	#
	# `/api/affiliates/*` is the public sign-up surface and `/api/affiliate/*` is
	# an affiliate's own dashboard — both are open to the internet by design and
	# do their own throttling and signature checks. `/affiliate*` is the page
	# itself, which is what the links inside our emails point at: miss it here and
	# every sign-in link a customer clicks lands on the video app's 404.
	#
	# `/api/partner/*` and `/partner*` are the names everything uses now. The
	# `/affiliate` pair is kept because links already emailed to people point at
	# it — the rename happened because ad and tracker blockers match "affiliate"
	# in a URL and cancel the request, which the sign-up form could only report
	# as "Failed to fetch".
	@licence path /api/licence/* /api/admin/* /api/partner/* /api/affiliate/* /api/affiliates/* /api/site-config /webhooks/stripe /admin /partner /partner/* /affiliate /affiliate/*
	handle @licence {
		reverse_proxy licence:8600
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy app:8501
	}

	request_body {
		max_size 5GB
	}
	encode gzip
}

# The licence and affiliate API, on a hostname meant to be proxied by
# Cloudflare (orange cloud). See API_DOMAIN in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Why a second hostname rather than proxying the one above. Cloudflare's free
# plan caps a request body at 100 MB, and this server accepts video uploads
# measured in gigabytes — putting the whole site behind it would trade one
# broken thing for another. So the video app keeps the direct hostname, and the
# small-payload API gets one that rides a network nobody blocks.
#
# **Only the licence service is reachable here**, deliberately. A hostname whose
# whole point is that it is small and always reachable should not also be a way
# to post five gigabytes at the video app.
{$API_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy licence:8600
	encode gzip
}
